g=A.p([0,"START WITH THE THREE STATEMENTS\n\nThe income statement answers: did the business earn a profit during this period? The balance sheet answers: what resources and obligations exist at the reporting date? The cash-flow statement answers: where did cash come from and where did it go? They are connected, not interchangeable.\n\nWORKED NUMERICAL BRIDGE\nA distributor reports revenue of \u20b9100 lakh, expenses of \u20b988 lakh and profit of \u20b912 lakh. During the same period, trade receivables increase by \u20b98 lakh, inventory increases by \u20b95 lakh, trade payables increase by \u20b93 lakh and depreciation of \u20b92 lakh is included in expenses. A simplified operating-cash bridge is: profit \u20b912 lakh + depreciation \u20b92 lakh \u2212 receivables increase \u20b98 lakh \u2212 inventory increase \u20b95 lakh + payables increase \u20b93 lakh = operating cash \u20b94 lakh. This is a teaching bridge; a full cash-flow statement must also test tax, interest, provisions and other non-cash or working-capital movements.\n\nHOW TO INVESTIGATE THE \u20b91 LAKH BANK BALANCE\n1. Confirm opening and closing bank balances against bank statements. 2. Reconcile cash movements and identify transfers, financing and investing flows. 3. Trace the \u20b98 lakh receivables movement to customer ageing and subsequent receipts. 4. Trace the \u20b95 lakh inventory movement to purchases, stock records and valuation. 5. Verify the payable movement against supplier statements and payment runs. 6. Explain the difference between operating cash and closing bank cash using all other cash-flow categories.\n\nCOMMON ERRORS\nCalling profit \u201ccash\u201d; treating an increase in receivables as a cash inflow; ignoring inventory; using unreconciled ledger balances; and assuming one ratio proves financial health.\n\nCHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nIf receivables rise by \u20b94 lakh while all else stays constant, operating cash is lower by \u20b94 lakh. Explain why: revenue may have been recognised before cash collection.",1,"WORKING CAPITAL AND THE CASH CONVERSION CYCLE\n\nWorking capital is current assets less current liabilities. The cash conversion cycle estimates how long cash is tied up between paying for inputs and collecting from customers: DIO + DSO \u2212 DPO. DIO measures inventory days, DSO receivable days and DPO payable days. Use consistent period lengths and clearly documented average balances.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nAssume DIO = 42 days, DSO = 38 days and DPO = 30 days. Cash conversion cycle = 42 + 38 \u2212 30 = 50 days. If process improvements reduce DIO to 37 and DSO to 33 while DPO remains 30, the cycle becomes 40 days: a 10-day reduction. Do not translate this automatically into cash savings; estimate the actual cash impact using relevant daily cost or sales bases and validate operational constraints.\n\nACTION PLAN\n1. Segment inventory by movement and obsolescence. 2. Fix billing accuracy and dispute causes before simply pressuring customers. 3. Improve cash application so receipts are allocated promptly. 4. Forecast supplier payments within agreed terms. 5. Track service levels, stock-outs, customer retention and supplier continuity alongside cash metrics.\n\nPRACTICE\nA business has DIO 55, DSO 46 and DPO 35. Calculate its CCC (66 days). Recommend one safe action for each component and name a risk guardrail.",2,"RECONCILIATIONS AND CONTROL DESIGN\n\nA reconciliation compares two independently maintained records that should agree, explains differences and documents resolution. A control is not complete merely because someone ticks a box. It needs a purpose, source data, frequency, preparer, reviewer, tolerance, evidence, escalation and closure.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nThe AP subledger is \u20b99.80 crore while the GL control account is \u20b910.00 crore. The difference is \u20b920 lakh. Do not post a balancing journal simply to make the figures agree. Split the difference into aged timing items, unposted interface batches, manual journals, mapping errors, cut-off issues and genuine omissions. For each item record amount, source reference, date, owner, root cause, expected resolution and reviewer sign-off.\n\nCONTROL WALKTHROUGH\n1. Confirm both reports use the same entity, currency and cut-off. 2. Check report completeness and extraction timestamps. 3. Reconcile opening balance plus movements to closing balance. 4. Match individual reconciling items to source evidence. 5. Investigate unusual, old or round-sum items. 6. Obtain independent review. 7. Track aged items until evidence-backed closure.\n\nPRACTICE\nDesign a monthly reconciliation control for AP-to-GL. Include at least eight fields in the evidence log and define when an item must be escalated.",3,"APPLIED FINANCIAL STATEMENT ANALYSIS\n\nAnalysis combines trend, common-size, ratio and cash-flow review. Never interpret a ratio without its business model, seasonality, accounting policy and peer context. Start with a question: what changed, why did it change, is it sustainable, and what decision follows?\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nRevenue rises from \u20b980 lakh to \u20b9100 lakh (25%). Operating profit rises from \u20b98 lakh to \u20b99 lakh (12.5%). Operating margin falls from 10% to 9%. The business grew, but profitability did not keep pace. Investigate price discounts, product mix, input costs, freight, overtime and one-off items. A revenue growth headline alone would hide margin pressure.\n\nANALYST METHOD\n1. Validate comparable periods and classifications. 2. Calculate absolute and percentage movement. 3. Build a bridge by volume, price, mix and cost. 4. Separate recurring from one-off effects. 5. Compare profit with operating cash and working capital. 6. State the risk, decision and evidence still required.\n\nPRACTICE\nRevenue increases 10%, cost of sales increases 16%, and operating expenses increase 5%. Explain the likely direction of gross and operating margins, then list the schedules needed to prove the cause.",4,"WORKING CAPITAL IMPROVEMENT WORKSHOP\n\nA working-capital programme is a cross-functional operating change, not a finance-only target. Establish a baseline, segment the opportunity, identify root causes, assign owners and protect service quality.\n\nILLUSTRATIVE CASE\nA company has \u20b960 lakh overdue receivables, \u20b925 lakh disputed invoices and \u20b915 lakh unapplied cash. These are not automatically additive opportunities: disputed invoices may already sit inside overdue receivables, and unapplied cash may relate to the same customer balances. Reconcile populations before estimating benefit.\n\n90-DAY PLAN\nDays 1\u201330: validate data, remove duplicate records, segment overdue balances, identify dispute categories and agree owners. Days 31\u201360: resolve high-value billing and delivery disputes, improve cash allocation, review credit holds and improve forecast discipline. Days 61\u201390: measure sustainable changes, audit control adherence and standardise successful routines.\n\nSUCCESS MEASURES\nTrack overdue value and count, DSO, dispute age, unapplied cash, promise-to-pay kept rate, forecast accuracy, stock-outs, supplier complaints and control exceptions. Define each KPI denominator and data source.\n\nPRACTICE\nPrepare a one-page proposal with baseline, root cause, action, owner, target, evidence, risk guardrail and review cadence. Explain how you will avoid double-counting benefits."],r,s)
