e=A.p([0,"PROCURE-TO-PAY: FROM REQUEST TO RECONCILED PAYMENT\\n\\nThe P2P process begins with a business need and ends only when the supplier liability and cash movement are correctly recorded. Typical stages are requisition, approval, purchase order, receipt, invoice capture, validation, matching, payment approval, bank execution and ledger reconciliation.\\n\\nWORKED THREE-WAY MATCH\\nA PO authorises 100 units at \u20b9500 each: \u20b950,000. The goods receipt confirms 96 accepted units: \u20b948,000. The supplier invoice is for 100 units: \u20b950,000. At a zero quantity tolerance, the invoice has a \u20b92,000 mismatch. The correct response is not to force-post the invoice. Confirm whether four units are pending, rejected, back-ordered or incorrectly invoiced; obtain receiving and procurement evidence; then approve only the supported amount or resolve the supplier invoice.\\n\\nCONTROL CHECKLIST\\nVerify supplier master approval, PO authority, receipt evidence, invoice uniqueness, tax arithmetic, tolerance rules, maker-checker approval, payment-file controls and bank-to-GL reconciliation. Keep rejected and blocked items visible with owner and age.\\n\\nPRACTICE\\nA duplicate invoice number appears for the same supplier and amount. Describe the checks to distinguish a true duplicate from a legitimate credit/rebill or separate legal entity invoice.",1,"ORDER-TO-CASH: BILLING, COLLECTION AND CASH APPLICATION\\n\\nO2C starts with a valid customer order and credit decision, then fulfilment, proof of delivery, billing, receivables, collections, dispute resolution, receipt and cash application. Collection effectiveness depends on correct invoices and clear dispute ownership\u2014not repeated reminders alone.\\n\\nWORKED RECEIPT CASE\\nCustomer A has three open invoices: \u20b940,000, \u20b925,000 and \u20b915,000. A bank receipt of \u20b950,000 arrives with remittance advice allocating \u20b940,000 to invoice 1 and \u20b910,000 to invoice 2. Apply only the evidenced allocation. Invoice 2 retains \u20b915,000; invoice 3 remains \u20b915,000. Do not allocate the remaining receipt to the oldest debt without support. If remittance is missing, keep the receipt in unapplied cash with date, bank reference, customer investigation owner and next action.\\n\\nDISPUTE TRIAGE\\nClassify billing, pricing, tax, delivery, quality, purchase-order and master-data disputes. Assign the issue to the function able to resolve it and track ageing, value, customer impact and evidence.\\n\\nPRACTICE\\nDesign a daily cash-application control that prevents duplicate allocation and ensures unapplied receipts are investigated within a defined service level.",2,"RECORD-TO-REPORT AND MONTH-END CLOSE\\n\\nR2R converts operational activity into controlled financial reporting. A close calendar sequences subledger completion, cut-off, accruals, prepayments, fixed assets, intercompany, reconciliations, review and reporting. A journal entry must have a business purpose, correct period, account, entity, support and approval.\\n\\nWORKED ACCRUAL\\nA company receives a December electricity service estimate of \u20b91,20,000, but the invoice arrives in January. If the service relates to December and the amount is reasonably estimable, record a December accrual: debit utility expense \u20b91,20,000; credit accrued expenses \u20b91,20,000. In January, reverse or clear it according to the accounting process and match the actual invoice. Investigate the \u20b910,000 difference if the final invoice is \u20b91,30,000; do not leave the estimate unreconciled.\\n\\nCLOSE CONTROL\\nUse a task owner, due date, dependency, evidence link, preparer, reviewer and escalation state. Reconcile subledgers to the GL and investigate aged suspense or unreconciled balances.\\n\\nPRACTICE\\nPrepare a five-day close sequence for AP, AR, bank, fixed assets and management reporting. Identify dependencies and the evidence required before sign-off.",3,"AP EXCEPTION MANAGEMENT AND ROOT-CAUSE ANALYSIS\\n\\nAn AP exception is a controlled work item, not simply a blocked invoice. Classify it by cause, value, age, supplier impact, financial close impact and responsible function. Common causes include PO mismatch, missing receipt, duplicate risk, tax error, invalid supplier master, approval gap and interface failure.\\n\\nWORKED PRIORITISATION\\nThree invoices are blocked: A \u20b98 lakh and 2 days old for missing receipt; B \u20b91 lakh and 45 days old for a tax mismatch; C \u20b94 lakh and 20 days old for duplicate risk. Prioritisation should not be value-only. Invoice C may pose duplicate-payment risk; B may be near close or statutory deadlines; A needs Receiving evidence. Set risk-based priorities and owners, and never bypass approval or matching controls to clear the queue.\\n\\nEXCEPTION LOG\\nCapture entity, supplier, invoice ID, amount, currency, reason code, first-seen date, age, owner, next action, due date, evidence, escalation and closure reviewer. Analyse recurring causes weekly.\\n\\nPRACTICE\\nBuild a root-cause Pareto from 100 fictional blocked invoices and propose two corrective actions with measurable outcomes and control safeguards.",4,"COLLECTIONS, CASH APPLICATION AND CUSTOMER DISPUTES\\n\\nSeparate overdue debt from unapplied cash, credit balances and disputed invoices. A customer may appear overdue because the receipt has not been allocated, not because the customer has failed to pay. Reconcile customer statements, bank receipts, remittance details and subledger postings before escalation.\\n\\nWORKED AGEING CASE\\nAn invoice of \u20b92,00,000 is 35 days overdue. The customer provides proof of a \u20b91,50,000 payment made yesterday, but the bank statement has not yet confirmed settlement. Keep the item under payment-in-transit investigation; do not mark it fully paid. Once \u20b91,50,000 is confirmed and applied, the remaining balance is \u20b950,000 and must be aged under the documented policy. Record the proof, bank reference, expected settlement, owner and follow-up date.\\n\\nCUSTOMER-SAFE ACTION\\nPrioritise high-value and high-risk accounts, resolve invoice disputes at source, record promises to pay, and escalate according to policy. Monitor kept-promise rate, dispute age, unapplied cash and repeat billing errors.\\n\\nPRACTICE\\nWrite a collections action plan for five accounts using value, age, dispute reason, payment evidence, owner and next contact date."],r,s)
d=A.p([0,"FINANCE DATA ANALYSIS WITH EXCEL\\n\\nBegin with a business question and define the record grain. For AP ageing, one row should normally represent one invoice or open invoice line, with a unique key, supplier, invoice date, due date, open amount, currency and status. Set one explicit report date so results are reproducible.\\n\\nWORKED FORMULA\\nAssume report date is in B1. For an unpaid invoice, overdue days can be calculated as MAX(0,$B$1-[@DueDate]); for a paid invoice or a blank due date, return a controlled status instead of a misleading number. Use SUMIFS to total open amount by supplier and COUNTIFS to count invoices by status. A blank due date should be flagged as a data-quality exception, not silently treated as current.\\n\\nVALIDATION TESTS\\nCheck duplicate invoice keys, blank supplier IDs, invalid dates, negative or zero balances, currency mismatches, closed invoices included in open balances and totals against the source control total. Test due today, due yesterday, future due date and blank due date.\\n\\nPRACTICE\\nCreate a 20-row fictional AP dataset, build overdue buckets and document five test cases with expected results.",1,"POWER QUERY: BUILD A REPEATABLE PIPELINE\\n\\nPower Query is a repeatable extract-transform-load workflow. Keep raw source files unchanged. Use documented steps to import, set data types, trim and clean text, standardise headers, map IDs, merge reference data, append monthly files and create exception queries.\\n\\nWORKED APPEND VS MERGE\\nJanuary and February invoice files have the same columns: append them to create one transaction table. A supplier master has one row per supplier and fields such as approved status and payment terms: merge it into the invoice table using SupplierID. Before merging, verify the key is unique in the master; otherwise one invoice may multiply into several rows. Keep unmatched supplier IDs in a separate exception output.\\n\\nREFRESH CONTROL\\nRecord source filenames and timestamps. Compare source row count to loaded rows plus explicitly excluded/rejected rows. Reconcile gross invoice value and open amount before and after transformation. Alert on missing columns, changed data types, duplicate master keys and unexpected nulls.\\n\\nPRACTICE\\nDesign a two-month AP refresh process and list the control totals, exception queries and ownership needed before a dashboard is refreshed.",2,"POWER BI MODEL AND KPI DESIGN\\n\\nA dashboard should answer a decision question, not merely display attractive charts. Define each KPI by business meaning, numerator, denominator, period, filters, source, owner and refresh cadence. Model facts at a declared grain and use dimensions such as date, supplier, entity, currency and exception reason. Avoid many-to-many relationships unless they are explicitly designed and tested.\\n\\nWORKED KPI\\nOverdue value = sum of open invoice balances where status is unpaid and due date is earlier than the report date. Overdue count is a different KPI: count of invoices meeting the same rule. Do not call overdue value divided by total invoice count an overdue rate. A value-based overdue rate uses overdue open value divided by total open value; a count-based rate uses overdue invoice count divided by total unpaid invoice count. Label them separately.\\n\\nRECONCILIATION AND UX\\nTie card totals to the source control total. Show report date, refresh time, filter context, excluded records and data-quality warnings. Provide drill-through to exception owner and source reference. Test totals with all filters and with each segment selected.\\n\\nPRACTICE\\nWrite a KPI dictionary for overdue value, overdue count, first-time match rate and average exception age. Specify the exact denominator and source for each.",3,"AP AGEING: FROM RAW FILE TO ACTION LIST\\n\\nAgeing is a decision tool for collections and supplier/customer risk, not just a bucket chart. Establish whether the report is for receivables or payables, define the as-of date, open-balance logic, due-date rule, credit-note treatment, partial-payment handling and bucket boundaries.\\n\\nWORKED AGEING\\nAn unpaid invoice of \u20b980,000 has a due date 12 days before the report date: it belongs in the 1\u201330 day overdue bucket. An invoice due exactly on the report date is not overdue under a rule that requires DueDate earlier than ReportDate. A credit note of \u20b910,000 must be handled according to the documented netting policy; do not simply drop it because it is negative. A partially paid invoice should age on its remaining open balance.\\n\\nACTIONABLE OUTPUT\\nInclude supplier/customer, invoice ID, due date, overdue days, open amount, currency, dispute reason, owner, next action and promise date. Reconcile bucket totals to the total open balance and investigate records that do not fit any bucket.\\n\\nPRACTICE\\nCreate five test invoices covering due today, 1 day overdue, 30 days overdue, partial payment and credit note. State the expected bucket and amount for each.",4,"DATA QUALITY, GOVERNED REFRESH AND DASHBOARD OPERATIONS\\n\\nA dashboard is trustworthy only when its lineage, quality checks and refresh ownership are visible. Define source system, extraction timestamp, transformation version, mapping owner, approved exclusions and control totals. A successful refresh message does not prove that the dataset is complete or correct.\\n\\nWORKED REFRESH INCIDENT\\nYesterday\u2019s AP dashboard contained 12,450 rows and \u20b98.2 crore open value. Today\u2019s refresh contains 11,980 rows and \u20b97.6 crore. Before publishing, investigate the 470-row and \u20b960 lakh reduction. Check source file completeness, filters, schema changes, failed joins, duplicate removal, status mapping and report-date changes. Retain the prior successful snapshot and communicate a data-quality incident if unresolved.\\n\\nOPERATING CONTROL\\nUse a refresh checklist with source receipt, row counts, control totals, rejected rows, null/duplicate checks, mapping exceptions, reviewer sign-off and publish decision. Quarantine incomplete data rather than silently replacing a valid report with a partial one.\\n\\nPRACTICE\\nDraft a refresh runbook defining owner, schedule, validation thresholds, escalation path, rollback approach and stakeholder notification."],r,s)
